window.docContents[207].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Synopsis--Purpose":{"contents":"State what must hold of the refined context as requirements that the build checks. Each Provenance.Requirement names the boundary it governs (a Provenance.Contract), the output port it is about, and its scope, the objects it quantifies over. R1 and R2 are scoped to the sort pairS: R1 is proved by a theorem, and R2 holds because no Assembles instance exists. R3 is scoped to pair P and is decided by a gate function over every state of P. The coverage census lists every output port of every boundary with the requirements that govern it, and #kind_requirement_clean fails if any port is ungoverned or unchecked.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L6 — What must hold\u0009Synopsis","header":"5.7.1.1. Purpose","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Synopsis--Purpose"},"/#annotation-pkc-math":{"contents":"/-- The vegetation attenuation `τ = exp(−2·b·ndvi)`, kind `attenuationK`. -/\n@[pkc_math]\ndef attenuationQ (cfg : AvsConfig α) (b : Quantity paramB α)\n    (ndvi : Quantity vegetationIndex α) : Quantity attenuationK α := …\n\n\nThe effect, appended to that declaration's docstring and typeset on its doc-gen4 page:\n\n\n\nTwo clauses control the shape. keeping binds a definition's lets as named auxiliary equations\nbelow the main one instead of inlining them — right when a model shares one subexpression across\nseveral outputs, where inlining both explodes the LaTeX and hides the structure the author wrote.\nsubstituting adds a derivation: one further equation per clause, each inlining the named\nhelpers, so a reader sees the equation as written and then the same equation expanded.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Using the library: annotations and generated indexes\u0009How a definition reads — the rendering family","header":"33.8.1. @[pkc_math]  — render this definition","id":"/#annotation-pkc-math"}});